+**Third v2 SFT verification — 2026-11-17**
+
+**Final decision: NO-GO for both LFM3-VL-2B and Qwen3.5-2B on `836e401958a62a25171f8a7bf7b1ec91727a6b6c`, at the requested v1 budget of 1,250 × 32 = 40,000 row presentations.** Two conversion defects remain: the new submit classifier deletes non-submit actions and retains their dependent observations; the scroll converter accepts clock-hand and text-selection-handle drags that the unchanged executor cannot represent faithfully. These are demonstrated in published examples, separately from the small amount of residual source-label noise in the random review. The latter would not, by itself, justify rejecting this SFT experiment.
+
+Audited dataset: [experimentalmachines/AndroidAgent-SFT-v2 at the immutable DATA revision](https://huggingface.co/datasets/experimentalmachines/AndroidAgent-SFT-v2/tree/836e401958a62a25171f8a7bf7b1ec91727a6b6c). Baseline: [second report](codex_data_v2_verify.md). Inspected `git diff e336f6d~1 -- data/build_v2_mix.py` and the current files, including the revision-3 header. Local HEAD was `325e78f6cd8ec70cedeeca41467c0bd9664cdce3`; the audited builder SHA-256 is `b1ba0583113c116c41811ea492ba0a59ed5fbc1a9acaf79361e611501ea35199`. No builder, trainer, job definition or dataset was changed, and no training/device run was launched.
+
+**Scope and provenance.** Downloaded exactly four train shards, **00002, 00005, 00009, 00013**, indices unused in either prior audit, plus **all three dev shards**, pinned stats and pinned README. All seven parquet files match the pinned Hub LFS SHA-256 values. Total downloaded data/card/stats: **2,099,056,962 bytes**. Reused the requested cache and raw-source annotations; fetched missing Odyssey annotation JSONs, not additional train shards. Evidence is isolated under `results/codex/data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/`.
+
+| Split/source | Independently checked rows | Full published count |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| train / AndroidControl | 14,052 | 55,936 |
+| train / GUI-Odyssey | 2,448 | 9,966 |
+| train / AMEX | 1,844 | 7,469 |
+| train / ScreenQA | 1,498 | 5,995 |
+| dev_odyssey | 1,000 | 1,000 |
+| dev_amex | 491 | 491 |
+| dev_screenqa | 500 | 500 |
+| **Total** | **21,833: 19,842 train + 1,991 dev** | **81,357: 79,366 train + 1,991 dev** |
+
+The downloaded stats equal `results/data_v2/stats.json` byte for byte. All four source-stage final log objects match their stats; no build log has a traceback. The full action counters total **81,357**, exactly the declared train/dev population. Those counters are **builder-side evidence**, not an independent examination of the other twelve train shards. Hub HEAD `2840951ea23475ba7db49bb11c00ec42361760d0` differs from the pin only in README; all data and stats blob identities agree. The README at the DATA pin still has revision-2 prose, so the updated local card was also inspected. Its Odyssey scroll-cutoff count says 1,282, while stats/logs say 1,279; this documentation discrepancy does not affect the decision. [Download manifest](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/download_manifest.json), [Hub inventory](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/inventory.json), [build/log/input-hash evidence](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/build_evidence.json).
+
+**Per-item disposition.** PASS means the specified defect passed the stated checks; it is not a certification of every source label.
+
+| Item | Disposition | Evidence and limits |
+|---|---|---|
+| **F1 — train/dev screen leakage** | **PASS** | Recomputed JPEG hashes and decoded RGB-pixel hashes for every downloaded image. Across all 19,842 sampled train and all 1,991 dev rows: **0 JPEG overlaps, 0 pixel overlaps, 0 source-qualified episode overlaps**. Every stored `img_sha` matches the bytes. Builder reports 30 Odyssey + 4 AMEX train exclusions and zero full byte-hash overlap. ScreenQA source train/validation image IDs are disjoint. Perceptual similarity and sibling task templates were not exhaustively tested. |
+| **F2 — type/submit contract** | **PARTIAL; BLOCKER B1** | Actual builder-loop fixtures correctly merge TYPE/TEXT + submit tap, require the immediately preceding raw type step, and stop at a second submit-like tap. The four exact Odyssey keyboard-submit cases from report 2 are now merged. AMEX has 477 explicit Enter and 34 tap merges; Odyssey has 3,103 tap merges. However, `keyboard_submit()` also treats **any occurrence of “keyboard”** as positive submit evidence, including toolbar, hide-keyboard and suggestion actions. Published dependent rows survive these false merges; see B1. |
+| **F3 — region-specific gestures** | **FAIL; BLOCKER B2** | The original dropdown example is rejected. All **482 AMEX + 247 Odyssey** sampled scroll targets agree with source endpoints and the current band rule. But targeted visual review finds **two clock-hand drags and one text-selection-handle drag** passing that rule and emitted as generic scrolls. Matching the converter does not establish executor equivalence. See B2. |
+| **F4 — key names / empty text** | **PASS for new sources** | All sampled new-source targets and **2,275 historical text actions** pass the key/blank and current near-miss checks. Original bad-text trajectories still truncate before their offending suffixes. Current assertions intentionally exempt AC from the key-name heuristic because ordinary human queries such as “home” can be valid text; this is a reasonable scope correction. |
+| **F5 — unreliable terminal success/infeasibility** | **PASS** | **0 Odyssey/AMEX terminal targets or terminal history entries** in the downloaded population. Full counters contain status only for AC: **5,998 rows**. The sample contains **1,495 AC complete** targets and no other status targets. Actual source-loop fixtures quarantine terminal markers. This eliminates the previous new-source false-completion mechanism; AC terminal truth was not exhaustively visually re-audited. |
+| **F6 — information goals ending silently** | **PASS for the reported defect** | Removing all new-source terminal targets also removes the silent-completion labels for factual requests. Their earlier navigation actions remain useful next-action supervision; they do not certify an answer or success. ScreenQA supplies explicit `answer` targets. New-source completion/answer coverage is reduced, deliberately. |
+| **F7 — intent/entity/QA contradictions** | **PARTIAL; residual source noise is nonblocking** | The reviewed ScreenQA `rico10893/10963` is explicitly excluded; builder counts one exclusion and it is absent from the sample. The wrong e-mail trajectory still truncates at AMEX step 8. Odyssey reconstruction matches the new verb/region blanking in every sampled current/history intent; **527/3,448** sampled Odyssey current intents are blank. This heuristic cannot validate named controls or facts. The random review finds one remaining Odyssey intent error and one ScreenQA answer error, detailed below. Original candidate instructions such as the Clock/plus-button mismatch can still pass the broad verb/region test; the exact offending target was not sampled this time. |
+| **F8 — AW-app exclusions/provenance** | **PASS for observed exclusions; coverage limit remains** | All **14,052 AC** episode IDs join to official train; all **3,448 Odyssey** rows join to the official split and permitted source app labels; all **2,335 AMEX** rows match the hash split and permitted source packages; all **1,998 ScreenQA** rows join to source questions/answers and permitted Rico packages. No sampled prompt has an excluded alias. The documented shared-built-in policy is acceptable. AC still lacks per-step package provenance, so these checks do not certify every screenshot against the 14-family exclusion policy. |
+| **F9 — quota/episode caps** | **PASS** | Adversarial one-episode fixture returns **3 unique rows**, respecting the cap across quota passes. Reconstructed all **24,329 AMEX train candidates**, obtaining **7,476 pre-image selections**, maximum 3/episode; every sampled AMEX train row belongs to that selection. Three frame drops and four dev-image exclusions explain the final **7,469**. Observed train maxima: Odyssey 3, AMEX 3, ScreenQA 2; dev maxima: Odyssey 1, AMEX 4. No cap violations. |
+| **F10 — AMEX boilerplate / empty intents** | **PASS for both model formats** | All 2,335 sampled AMEX current intents and all their historical intents are blank. Refreshed LFM3's actual template at model revision `23387beda43c60310348e83e25f78f33c3140d3a`; it is unchanged from audit 2. LFM3 and the checked-in Qwen template both keep empty and nonempty intent plus action inside the assistant generation span, and training text starts with the thinking-enabled inference prefix. Qwen's empty think contains whitespace. This is a character-span/message-shape check, not a GPU/tokenizer-mask test. |
+| **F11 — exact duplicates** | **PASS** | **0 exact image+messages duplicates and 0 duplicate row identities** in sampled train. Five excess image+action rows have differing messages, so they are not exact duplicates. Full merge reports 64 exact exclusions. |
+| **F12 — AMEX frame mismatch** | **PASS** | All 2,335 sampled AMEX rows agree with source-device orientation and aspect ratio; no sampled history references a declared landscape frame. Reconstructed pre-image selection still includes the three identified mismatched-frame rows, and the builder drops exactly three. This does not independently validate arbitrary coordinate transforms beyond the checked frame contract. |
+| **Issue 1 — OCR failure handling** | **PASS; dormant for this mix** | Actual worker fixtures reject nonzero exit, empty/whitespace OCR and two-character output as `terminal_ocr_failed`; pending text is rejected as `terminal_pending_screen`. No new-source terminals remain, so OCR is not the reason F5/F6 pass. No live Tesseract OCR run was needed or claimed. |
+| **Issue 2 — overbroad typo filtering** | **MAIN FIX PASSES; minor coverage defect remains** | The real “Chocolate chip cookies” and “properties of cube” query fixtures now pass; the e-mail typo still fails. Matching is restricted to quotes/e-mails. Two synthetic boundaries still over-filter: a correct e-mail followed by sentence punctuation in the goal, and an exact later quoted alternative preceded by a similar alternative. These discard valid candidates rather than fabricate labels; no incidence estimate for the full build is claimed. Nonblocking for this pin. |
+| **Issue 3 — quotas/shortfall and header** | **ACCEPTABLE** | Header now describes the revision-3 policy. Final train is **79,366**, short of 80,000 by **634 (0.79%)**; quotas are ceilings. New-source train scrolls total **2,295**; only **13 standalone Enter** targets remain. There is no need to manufacture filler or increase the step budget. |
+| **Issue 4 — AC landscape rows** | **PASS** | AC stage now filters portrait geometry before selection and counts seven excluded v1 rows. **All 21,833 downloaded images are portrait**, decode correctly and match the shared resize rule. |
+| **Issue 5 — revision pinning** | **LOAD PATH PASSES; metadata caveat** | Both jobs specify this immutable revision in `TRAIN_DATA` and `DEV_REV`. Executed the real training/eval loader bodies with recording stubs: both the Hub file listing and `load_dataset` receive the pin. Pipeline dev counting/generation also propagates it. However, `_provenance()` at `train/sft_vlm.py:63` still records repository HEAD, ignoring `#revision`; correct this diagnostic field. The actual loading remains pinned and the full argument is recorded, so this logging bug is not a content blocker. |
+
+Primary check outputs: [independent image/schema/hash checks](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/checks.json), [AMEX/source/cap reconstruction](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/provenance.json), [Odyssey reconstruction](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/odyssey_provenance.json), [terminal/intent inventory](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/semantic_inventory.json), [actual-loop/executor/OCR/loader probes](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/rule_probes.json), [both template checks](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/template_render.json), [extra checks](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/extra_checks.json).
+
+**B1 — the new submit predicate erases real actions (training blocker).** `data/build_v2_mix.py:132–139` matches `keyboard` anywhere in the model-generated instruction, OR a bottom-right point. The Odyssey loop at lines 351–357 consumes that tap before intent consistency filtering. Raw adjacency and the second-tap cutoff work; they do not establish that the first tap is a submit. The actual-loop fixture `TEXT → “Tap to hide the keyboard” → result tap` wrongly consumes the hide action. The real executor probe confirms that the replacement `input_text` executes `type_text` plus one Enter, not an arbitrary toolbar or back-button action.
+
+The strongest published counterexample is **train/odyssey/5994133452263654/31**. Source step 26 types `Narcissus` into WPS. Step 27 taps **[686,499]**, described as an icon in the toolbar “above the keyboard”; it opens image selection. The classifier merges it. The published step-31 image shows the Download image picker with the Narcissus photo selected, while its retained source history is **22,23,24,25,26,28,29,30**: text entry is followed directly by controls inside the image picker, with no action opening it. Pressing Enter in the document is not that missing toolbar tap. The [pinned published picker](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/visual/merge_04.png), [saved row/raw-step evidence](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/blocking_examples.json), and [cached earlier step-28 picker image](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/visual/old_wps_after_toolbar.png) make the omission reviewable. The last image is explicitly corroboration from audit 1, not a newly downloaded third-revision row.
+
+Additional overmatches needing review include **4912446279173881/4**, a tap at **[249,453]** consumed on “suggestion … keyboard” wording: the [published next observation, step 5](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/visual/merge_06.png), is an opened **Merck** article, while history has only typed `Zoom`. The intervening tap is gone; whether Enter could independently open that article was not device-tested. **6951686067814574/4**, explicitly “Minimize the keyboard” at **[118,971]**, is also consumed before the [published Generate action](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/visual/merge_05.png). Keyboard hiding is not established as equivalent to Enter. These are supporting cases; the WPS missing image-picker action is the decisive example. A cached Simplenote back-button image likewise confirms that a copied instruction mentioning keyboard can consume a navigation tap.
+
+Among 2,978 reconstructed Odyssey annotation files, **1,858** submit merges were encountered; **108** were outside the bottom-right corner and matched on instruction text alone, with published later rows for **39** such events. **These are candidate counts, not 108 proven errors or an unbiased error-rate estimate.** The WPS example already demonstrates a generator defect affecting retained training history. Tighten submit recognition to positive submit evidence; do not equate mentions of a keyboard, its toolbar, suggestions or dismissal with Enter. Where equivalence is ambiguous, cut the episode rather than deleting a real action and retaining its suffix. [Text-only merge inventory](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/text_only_merges.json).
+
+**B2 — accepted drags are not generic scrolls (training blocker).** The band filter at `data/build_v2_mix.py:95–107` tests geometry, not the operation performed by the gesture. Review of 16 deliberately short/off-centre accepted scrolls found these concrete failures:
+
+| Published row | Recorded source gesture | Published target and defect |
+|---|---|---|
+| **train/odyssey/4394352513523177/22** | **[507,459] → [389,468]**, dragging the selected minute hand from 00 toward 55 in a Clock dial | `scroll right`; executor swipes **[500,500] → [0,500]**, not the recorded hand adjustment. [Overlay](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/visual/scroll_odyssey_06.png) |
+| **dev_odyssey/odyssey/0173202679441530/21** | **[487,454] → [386,469]**, the same minute-hand operation | Same generic target; blanking its incorrect intent does not preserve the gesture. [Overlay](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/visual/scroll_odyssey_02.png) |
+| **train/odyssey/4143524179769456/4** | **[291,643] → [580,746]**, starting on the blue text-selection handle after a long press | `scroll left`; executor swipes **[500,500] → [1000,500]**, away from the handle. The source's following action copies the selected text. [Overlay](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/visual/scroll_odyssey_08.png) |
+
+All coordinates above are on the source 0–1000 grid; overlays scale both paths onto the published image. No exact post-gesture clock value or device failure rate is claimed. The visible widgets, source paths and actual executor command establish the lost operation. These are a systematic action-abstraction problem, not merely a noisy task description. With the v1 action space/executor unchanged, quarantine these control/selection drags and dependent suffixes; alternatively, an explicit drag action would require a different executor/data contract. Merely documenting the band test as heuristic does not make these known examples compatible. The other 13 targeted scrolls produced no further confirmed conversion failure. [Target manifest](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/target_samples.json), [raw contexts](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/blocking_examples.json).
+
+**History reconstruction and structural results.** Rebuilt complete supplied histories from raw annotations for **all 3,448 Odyssey + 2,335 AMEX rows**: zero construction, action or intent mismatches and zero continuations past the current semantic cutoff rules. **906 Odyssey and 170 AMEX** downloaded rows occur after a submit merge. History numbers are retained-action numbers, not raw `step_idx`; the verifier accounts for this in both sources. Another **8,029 AC cross-row history links** agree. All 21,833 rows have one decodable image, the expected prompt/message structure, canonical strict action JSON equal to `gt_action`, and correct resize dimensions. This proves implementation agreement, while B1 shows that the implemented transformation itself is sometimes wrong. Image filtering occurs after history construction; this bounded audit is not a proof about every unobserved materialization drop.
+
+**Random visual review and acceptable residual noise.** Reviewed **32 randomly selected train rows per new source**, without replacement from sorted IDs, using integer RNG seeds **20261131 / 20261132 / 20261133**. All twelve contact sheets were viewed at original size. Also viewed 24 targeted pinned rows (16 scroll overlays and eight merge-related rows), two cached earlier-revision corroborating images, and enlarged ambiguous random examples. The targeted findings are kept outside the random noise estimate.
+
+| Source | Random rows | Locally plausible | Clear source-label error | Uncertain | Estimated clear-error rate in this sample |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| GUI-Odyssey | 32 | 30 | 1 | 1 | **3.1%** |
+| AMEX | 32 | 31 | 0 | 1 | **0% observed**, not evidence of zero population noise |
+| ScreenQA | 32 | 29 | 1 | 2 | **3.1%** |
+| **Total, equally sampled sources** | **96** | **90** | **2** | **4** | **2.1% clear; 6.25% if all uncertain rows were errors** |
+
+The two clear random errors are ordinary source supervision errors:
+
+- **Odyssey `6532382455851741/11`** types `10 mins per day` into the Todoist **Description** field under an existing **Chinese Learning** title, while its intent says to type the task name and due date. The action is locally useful; the intent is wrong. [Image](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/visual/odyssey_22.png).
+- **ScreenQA `rico11000/11117`** answers **Read** to “Which tab is selected?”, although **Unread (1)** is the selected light tab and Read is dark. [Image](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/visual/screenqa_21.png).
+
+Uncertain examples include a Repeat control for a task due this month, an advertised app versus host-app name, branding used as a developer name, and a Facebook viewer gesture. They were not silently counted as correct or as definite errors. The 95% Wilson intervals are approximately **0.6–15.7% for 1/32**, and **0–10.7% for 0/32**; clustering and the bounded shard sample further limit generalization. These are local annotation judgments, not task-success rates, whole-mix noise estimates or an AC quality audit. My judgment is that this observed residual source noise is acceptable for an exploratory 40,000-presentation SFT comparison. It is **not** the basis for NO-GO. [Complete 96-row ledger](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/visual_review.md), [machine-readable judgments](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/visual_review.json), [random-selection manifest](data_v2_cache/verify3_836e401958/visual_samples.json).
+
+**Training budget and final gate.** Both jobs preserve 1,250 optimizer steps, batch size 8, accumulation 4, LR `2e-4`, LoRA rank 64/alpha 128 and 774,400-pixel budget. On one GPU this is **40,000 row presentations**, approximately **0.504 passes** through this mix. Uniform sampling gives expected presentations of approximately **28,191 AC, 5,023 Odyssey, 3,764 AMEX and 3,021 ScreenQA**; the 13 Enter rows yield only **6.6 expected presentations**. No epoch or row-budget compensation is warranted. Equal steps/rows does not establish equal token compute or accuracy.
+
+The pin propagation and both templates pass the scoped checks. No GPU training, on-device replay or downstream accuracy claim follows from this audit. Resolve **B1 (false submit merges)** and **B2 (control/selection drags mapped to scroll)**, preserve valid histories by cutting dependent suffixes where needed, rebuild and pin the resulting bytes. Fixing the two isolated random-source labels is useful cleanup, but it is not a demand for noise-free SFT data. **Final verdict for this exact revision and v1 recipe: LFM3-VL-2B — NO-GO; Qwen3.5-2B — NO-GO.**
+
+Reproduction scripts: [bounded downloads](data_v2_verify3_download.py), [schema/image/hash checks](data_v2_verify3_checks.py), [rule extraction](data_v2_verify3_rules.py), [actual-loop/executor/loader/OCR probes](data_v2_verify3_probes.py), [AMEX/source reconstruction](data_v2_verify3_provenance.py), [Odyssey reconstruction](data_v2_verify3_odyssey.py), [both template checks](data_v2_verify3_templates.py), [random visuals](data_v2_verify3_visuals.py), [targeted visuals](data_v2_verify3_targets.py), [human review ledger](data_v2_verify3_ledger.py). Scripts use the existing audit Python dependencies and HF cache. Preserve the old audit directories when reproducing.
